# Analysis Documents

`context/analysis/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug>.md` hold investigation and
implementation plans. They are important while open, less once implemented.

## Create

Create a new analysis with the CLI (scaffolds the dated path and frontmatter):

```bash
sdt context new --type analysis --title "<title>" [--objective <slug>] [--prior-art]
```

Timestamps (`created`/`updated`) come from `sdt time iso` (RFC3339 UTC).
Or hand-write the file from the template below.

## Purpose

- Record a reasoning/decision process: what was examined, what was decided, why.
- Every analysis has a clear objective that triggered it.

## When to start here (intent first)

A new objective outside an existing analysis/plan starts here: create the
analysis and stop, then proceed plan → tasks → execution only with explicit
approval — see AGENTS.md (HARD RULE 1).

## Structure

```markdown
---
kind: analysis
title: "<one-line title>"
summary: "<1-2 sentence summary — MANDATORY, index source>"
context: "<objectives / what triggered the analysis>"
objective: <slug>         # optional: kebab-case group key; same slug in every
                          # analysis targeting the same objective
topics: [<topic>, ...]    # optional: controlled subjects from context/topics.yaml
entities: [<entity>, ...] # optional: named things the analysis mentions
categories: [<category>, ...] # optional: work type from context/categories.yaml
                          # (bug, issue, new-feature, feature-change, refactor, improvement, research)
status: active            # active | draft | completed | archived — statuses defined in the matrix (architecture/stack.md)
created: "<ISO 8601>"
updated: "<ISO 8601>"
links:                    # optional related documents
  - decisions/0002-api-format
sources:                  # optional: analysis/plan this derives from or extends
  - analysis/<date>-<slug>.md
project: <project>
agent: <agent/tool>          # optional
model: <model id>            # optional
session: <session id>        # optional
---

## Problem statement
## Current state
## Constraints             # optional: fixed non-negotiables
## Assumptions & Unknowns
## Non-goals
## Investigation
## Behaviour decisions     # optional: rule + concrete example + decider
## Design decisions        # optional: technical choice + rationale
## Options considered
### Option A: <name>
### Option B: <name>
## Plans                   # optional: ordered wave plans derived from this analysis (R2)
## Postponed from         # status: postponed only: origin option + why not now + revival condition
## Evidence / sources
## Risks and unknowns
## Open questions
## Recommendation
## Next steps
```

## Section descriptions

- **Problem statement** — what triggered this analysis: a bug, a decision, a
  design question. Be concrete: symptoms observed, not just "investigate X".
- **Current state** — facts as they are today, no opinions. Verifiable by anyone
  re-reading later without re-doing the investigation: code paths, data, logs,
  error messages, versions.
- **Assumptions & Unknowns** — mandatory, do not skip. Anything taken for
  granted goes here with a confidence level (high/medium/low) and the evidence.
  **Rule**: an assumption with confidence `low` that materially affects the
  recommendation must be surfaced as an open question, not folded silently into
  the conclusion.
- **Non-goals** — what this analysis deliberately does not cover, to keep it
  bounded.
- **Constraints** (optional) — fixed non-negotiables (mandated stack, standards,
  explicit exclusions) captured once, usually by a `>scope` Constitution phase.
  Distinct from assumptions: a constraint is a decision already taken, not a
  guess.
- **Investigation** — what was tried, checked, tested. Include dead ends; they
  save the next person (human or agent) from repeating them. Format:
  `<step taken> → <result / finding>`, incl. "ruled out because ...".
- **Behaviour decisions** (optional) — each rule with its concrete example and
  who decided it (user answer or a declared default). A rule without its example
  is the ambiguity this section exists to remove.
- **Design decisions** (optional) — technical choices with their rationale,
  filled by the `>scope` Plan phase; the plan's Design contract references them
  and may only change one with an explicit note.
- **Options considered** — only if the analysis leads to a choice; otherwise
  omit. One subsection per option: Description, Pros, Cons, Effort/risk, and
  `**Outcome:**` (accepted / rejected / postponed) — R3.
- **Postponed from** — `status: postponed` analyses only: the origin option, the
  reason it was not chosen now, and the condition that would revive it (R3).
- **Plans** — the ordered list of wave plans derived from this analysis, one line
  each (number, slug, one-sentence scope, analysis objectives covered) when the
  analysis feeds more than one plan (R2).
- **Evidence / sources** — links, benchmark numbers, upstream issues, docs that
  support the findings. Prefer primary sources.
- **Risks and unknowns** — what remains uncertain even after this analysis.
  Distinguish from open questions: risks nobody can answer yet (e.g. depends on
  production data we don't have); open questions a specific person must decide.
- **Open questions** — points requiring a human decision before proceeding. If
  more than 2-3 or they need tracking over time, move them to a dedicated
  questions doc and link via `sources` instead of duplicating here.
- **Recommendation** — the conclusion, stated plainly, with confidence level
  (high/medium/low) and why. This is what a plan will be built on top of.
- **Next steps** — checkboxes: e.g. turn this into a plan for the fix, get open
  questions answered before planning.

## Rules

- **No H1 title** — body starts at H2; see AGENTS.md (document conventions).
- `summary` is mandatory and used by `sdt context reindex`.
- Timestamps: `created`/`updated` are UTC ISO 8601. `created` is written at
  creation (`sdt time iso`); `updated` is CLI-written — refresh a body-only edit
  with `sdt context touch`, never by hand.
- **CLI-only state**: never hand-edit `status:`, `updated:` or checkbox markers;
  use `sdt context status set`, `sdt context touch` and `sdt context check`.
- Dated files: integrate/modify the current analysis while it is the active one;
  a materially new line of investigation gets a new dated file (and sets
  `sources` back to the analysis it extends).
- **R1 — split a too-broad analysis into siblings, but only after asking.**
  Split when **any** of: it would carry more than one `## Options considered`
  group belonging to different domains; it would need more than one
  `Recommendation`; its sources span unrelated subjects; it would exceed what
  one agent session can hold while writing. Then **ask the user in a single
  question**, proposing the concrete split (file list + slugs + the shared
  `objective` + which sibling is the umbrella), and never create the sibling set
  unasked. When the intent is already covered by an active analysis, integrate in
  place (HARD RULE 1) and do not ask. **Sibling discipline**: distinct slugs and
  summaries (the near-duplicate lint flags careless ones), each sibling links to
  the umbrella, and the umbrella lists them in a `## Sibling analyses` section.
  `sdt context lint` raises a SUGGESTION past 12 sections or 6 options — the ask
  still comes first.
- **R3 — every option records its fate.** At the end of an analysis each option
  in `## Options considered` carries exactly one outcome: **accepted** (it
  becomes the `Recommendation` and the subject of a plan), **rejected** (one line
  with the reason, in the option's own subsection; a rejected approach found
  later during execution is a `note_type: dead-end` note), or **postponed** (a
  new analysis, see below). Record it as `- **Outcome:** <verdict> (<reason>)`
  in the option's subsection; `sdt context lint` raises a SUGGESTION on an option
  with none.
- **A postponed option becomes its own analysis, not a footnote.** Create one
  analysis per origin (split further only when the options belong to different
  domains, each then its own umbrella linking to the origin) with
  `status: postponed`, the **same `objective`**, `sources` pointing back at the
  origin, and a `## Postponed from` section naming the origin option, the reason
  it was not chosen now, and the **condition that would revive it**; link it from
  the origin's `links`. It is a valid, closed analysis — `summary`, `Non-goals`,
  no open points — stating the decision that is *not* taken yet. It is not a
  parking lot. Revival is a status flip plus a `## Revival` line (date, trigger,
  fresh `updated`) and a re-run of `--prior-art`; retrieve them with
  `sdt context list --type analysis --status postponed` (counted in
  `sdt context status`).
- `objective` (optional): kebab-case slug identifying the initiative; use the same
  slug in every analysis that targets the same objective so
  `sdt context reindex` and the viewer can group them.
- **Check the objective's dead ends first**: before opening an analysis for an
  objective, review its `note_type: dead-end` notes (`sdt context status`
  counts them; `sdt context reindex` groups them under the objective) and do
  not re-run a rejected approach without new evidence.
- `topics` / `entities` (optional): subjects and named things,
  **distinct from `objective`**. Topics come from the controlled list in
  `context/topics.yaml`; `sdt context lint` reports unknown topics
  (SUGGESTION, with the canonical form for aliases) and they feed search filters
  and prior-art detection.
- `categories` (optional, analyses only): the **kind of work** the analysis is,
  from the controlled register `context/categories.yaml` (bug, issue,
  new-feature, feature-change, refactor, improvement, research); multi-valued and
  distinct from `topics` (subject) and `objective` (initiative). Set it with
  `sdt context new --type analysis --category <slug>`; `sdt context lint` reports
  unknown categories (SUGGESTION, with the canonical form for aliases) and
  `context list`/`search` filter on them.
- **Find prior art before writing.** `sdt context new --type analysis --prior-art`
  searches the corpus on the objective/title/topics and proposes candidates in a
  `## Prior art` section (add `--prior-art-links` to pre-fill `links`).
  Review every candidate, keep or discard it, and never leave the auto-generated
  note in the final document.
- **Declare how it relates to prior work.** Set `links` (generic
  correlation), `supersedes` (this document replaces an older one) or
  `contradicts` (it rebuts it), or state `links: none` with a reason when
  there is genuinely no prior work. `sdt context lint` raises a SUGGESTION
  when an analysis declares nothing, and another when two analyses of the same
  `objective` have near-identical title/summary (possible duplicate — link
  or differentiate them).
- Leave **no open points**: if a decision is missing, ask on the fly or register
  it as an open question in `context/questions/`; keep the user in
  control of the decisions.
- **Map objectives to tasks.** List the objectives the work must satisfy; when
  the analysis feeds a plan, state which objective each phase/task covers, so
  every task file can carry a `## Design` study of those objectives (see
  `instructions/tasks.md`) and the plan's final Validation phase can verify
  them.
- Track work via the 5-stage development lifecycle — see AGENTS.md.
- Verify-step before finishing — see AGENTS.md (5-stage development lifecycle).

## Initial scan (bootstrap)

On an established (brownfield) or new (greenfield) project, do an initial scan
before changing anything: read the README, docs, code structure and `git log`.
Offer what to capture and ask confirmation before writing knowledge files
(non auto-capturing). Populate `architecture/` and new decisions in
`decisions/`; `worklog/` stays history.
